  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 1014 PM MDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nThere is good agreement thru the weekend that an elongated upper \nlevel trough will extend from the Pacific NW southward into \nCalifornia. This will allow for mainly SW flow aloft across the \narea thru Sun. Ahead of this trough, moisture from Rachel will \nmove northeast with the best PW's staying over wrn CO into the \nGreat Basin. Thus precip chances will stay on the low side across \nmuch of the area, except west of the Divide, where there will be a\na chc of showers and a few tstms each aftn. As far as temps, \nreadings will remain well above normal, across much of the area on\nSat, with highs in the mid to upper 80's over nern CO. For Sun, \ndepending on how much higher level cloud cover there is, readings \nmay end slightly cooler over the plains with highs in the lower \nto mid 80's.\n\nLooking ahead to Mon, an upper level low will develop over \nCalifornia and Nevada with SW flow aloft over the area. Once \nagain, any tropical moisture with the remnants of Rachel will stay\nwest of the area. Thus, any chc for showers/tstms will remain \nwest of the divide. Meanwhile, there is still some disagreement \nas to whether a cool front will backdoor into nern CO. The latest \ndata has it stalling out over the far nern plains. As a result, \nthis would lead to another day of above normal temps over much of \nthe area.\n\nBy Tue, the upper level low will move into the Great Basin with SW \nflow aloft remaining over the area. There is agreement that a \ncool front will move across nern CO Mon night into Tue. As a \nresult, should see highs drop back to more seasonal levels across \nthe plains. Meanwhile, as far as precip chances, once again it \nappears best chance will stay mainly to the west of the area with \nany shower/tstm activity focused west of the divide.\n\nFor Tue night thru Wed, there remains a lot of uncertainty on how \nquickly the upper level low will move to the ENE.  The GFS has this \nsystem moving from Utah into central WY by Wed aftn while the ECMWF \nkeeps the system stalled over Utah.  Overall, should see a gradual \nincrease in precip chances for the higher terrain late Tue night \ninto Wed, unless the system slows down even further.  In addition, \nwould likely see accumulating snow in the mtns as well. Across nern \nCO, it will be cooler as highs stay in the 60's with a good chc of \nshowers and a few tstms, especially on Wed.\n\nBy Thu, the ECMWF has an elongated weakening upper level low \nextending from sern WY into swrn CO.  Meanwhile, the GFS has the \nupper level low over central South Dakota.  If the ECMWF is correct \nthen it would remain cool and unsettled over the area.  On the other \nhand, the GFS solution would lead to dry conditions with seasonal \ntemperatures.",
